.TH CTPOOL_SHUTDOWN_IMMEDIATE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_shutdown_immediate \- immediate shutdown
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_shutdown_immediate(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Cancels every task in the queue, and then tells each worker thread to
exit once it finishes the task it is running, if it has one. The call
blocks until every worker thread has exited.

A cancelled task that ctpool_submit(3), ctpool_try_submit(3) or
ctpool_timed_submit(3) queued with an on_complete gets
on_complete(arg, false), and its fn never runs. Those calls run on the
thread that calls this function, before it returns, with no lock of
the pool held. From such an on_complete, ctpool_wait(3) and both
shutdown functions on the same pool are no-ops, and ctpool_destroy(3)
on the same pool is a fatal error; see ctpool_submit(3).

The future of a cancelled task is marked as done and as cancelled, and
every thread blocked in ctpool_future_get on a cancelled future is
woken up and gets NULL.

A second call, whether it comes after the first one or at the same
time on another thread, does no shutdown work of its own, but it still
blocks until the shutdown that is under way ends. So every caller gets
the same guarantee: once the call returns, every worker thread has
exited.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed
       handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A task that is already running when you make this call finishes as
usual.
Every submit function returns ccol_not_permitted after this call;
ctpool_submit_future returns NULL and stores ccol_not_permitted in its
rv_out.

If a ctpool_shutdown_drain is already running, this call escalates the
drain instead of simply joining it: it discards whatever is left in the
queue at that moment, cancels the futures of those tasks and calls
on_complete(arg, false) for each of them that has one, exactly as if
this call had started the shutdown. A task that the drain has already
started still finishes as usual.

A call to ctpool_shutdown_immediate on pool from inside a task running
on one of pool's worker threads, or from the on_complete callback of
that task, does nothing at all, for the reason ctpool_shutdown_drain
gives for the same case: a worker thread cannot join itself. The call
neither discards the queue nor blocks.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
